course: technology
lesson: 5
title: Clothes: T-shirts, jeans and trainers
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Look at the label in your T-shirt: it may say Made in Bangladesh or Vietnam, because many clothes are designed in one country, sewn in another where wages are lower, and shipped across the sea in containers.
explain: A T-shirt starts as fluffy cotton on a plant, which is cleaned, spun into thread, knitted into fabric, dyed, cut and sewn, and much of the sewing is still done by people at very fast machines.
explain: Jeans are woven with blue threads one way and white threads the other, which is why the inside is paler than the outside.
explain: A sewing machine does not tie knots like your hands: a needle pushes the top thread down and a hook below loops it round a second thread, thousands of times a minute.
explain: Cheap clothes have hidden costs: cotton needs lots of water, dye can pollute rivers, and in 2013 a clothes-factory building collapsed in Bangladesh, so people now push for safer factories and fair pay.
fact: Cotton fluff from the cotton plant is cleaned, spun into thread, knitted into fabric, dyed, cut and sewn into a T-shirt.
fact: Growing the cotton for one T-shirt often takes over two thousand litres of water.
fact: Denim for jeans is woven with blue-dyed threads one way and white threads the other, so the inside is paler; jeans are then cut, sewn and often washed to look worn.
fact: Metal rivets on jeans were patented (registered as an invention) in 1873 to stop pockets tearing on miners' work trousers.
fact: A sewing machine locks each stitch by looping the needle's top thread round a second thread from below; factory machines can make around five thousand stitches a minute, so fingers stay well away from the needle.
fact: Many clothes are designed in one country and made in another, such as Bangladesh or Vietnam, where wages are lower; Bangladesh is one of the biggest clothes exporters in the world.
fact: In 2013 the Rana Plaza clothes-factory building collapsed in Bangladesh, killing more than a thousand workers, and afterwards safety checks were made stricter.
fact: Trainers are built from dozens of pieces: moulded rubber or foam soles and cut or knitted tops, glued and stitched over a foot-shaped block called a last.
fact: Knitting machines have hundreds of tiny hooked needles that pull loops of yarn through other loops, the same idea as hand knitting but much faster.
fact: Wool comes from sheep that are usually sheared once a year, which, done properly, does not hurt them: it is like a haircut and keeps them cool in summer.
fact: Dyes bond to the fibres; the first indigo blue for jeans came from a plant, and dye factories must clean their water because some rivers near them have been badly polluted.
fact: A zip has two rows of teeth on cloth tapes, and the slider pushes them together so each tooth hooks into the gap opposite; its modern form dates from about 1913; a zip with a missing tooth keeps coming apart there, and zips should be pulled slowly with skin kept away from the teeth.
fact: Clothes given to clothes banks are sorted: good ones are resold, many are shipped abroad to markets, and worn ones become rags or stuffing.
fact: T-shirt pictures are printed by pushing ink through a fine mesh screen, one screen for each colour, or by a special printer that sprays ink straight onto the cloth; after many washes and stretches the printed ink layer can crack.
exercise: What is a cotton T-shirt made from? | Fluff from the cotton plant, spun into thread | Wool sheared from special cotton sheep on hot farms | Plastic pellets melted together | Paper pulp pressed very thin
exercise: About how much water can the cotton for one T-shirt need? | Over two thousand litres | About one small glass | About one bucket | About one teaspoon
exercise: Why is the inside of jeans paler than the outside? | Blue threads go one way and white threads the other | The inside fades from your legs rubbing against it all day | The factory simply forgets to dye the inside of the cloth | The inside is bleached on purpose so it feels softer on skin
exercise: Why do jeans have little metal rivets? | To stop pockets tearing, first on miners' trousers | To make the jeans heavy enough so they do not flap in the wind | To make the jeans stick to magnets | To show the size of the jeans
exercise: How does a sewing machine lock a stitch? | A hook below loops the top thread round a second thread | The needle ties a tiny knot in the thread with each stitch it makes | A drop of glue sets each stitch | Heat melts the two threads together
exercise: About how many stitches a minute can a factory sewing machine make? | Around five thousand | Around five | Around fifty | Around five million
exercise: Why are many clothes made in countries like Bangladesh or Vietnam? | Wages are lower there, so making clothes costs less | Cotton can only be sewn in hot countries | It is against the law to sew clothes anywhere in Europe now | Sewing machines only work near the sea
exercise: What happened at Rana Plaza in 2013? | A clothes-factory building collapsed, killing over a thousand workers | The first zip in the world was made there | A giant T-shirt was made for a world record | A factory began making jeans from paper
exercise: What is a last in a shoe factory? | A foot-shaped block that a trainer is built over | The final shoe made by the factory at the end of each day | A special rubber used for soles | The machine that threads the laces
exercise: How many pieces can one trainer be made of? | Dozens | Exactly two | Just one | Many millions
exercise: How does a knitting machine make a jumper? | Hooked needles pull loops of yarn through other loops | A shuttle weaves threads across and back on a loom | Wool fibres are glued together into a flat sheet and cut out | Melted wool is sprayed into a mould
exercise: Does shearing hurt a sheep? | No, done properly it is like a haircut | Yes, the wool is pulled out by the roots | Yes, the sheep must sleep for a week after | Yes, the sheep's skin comes off with the wool
exercise: Where did the first indigo blue for jeans come from? | A plant | A blue rock | Squid ink | Blueberries
exercise: Why must dye factories clean their dirty water? | Dye water can badly pollute rivers | Clean water makes brighter colours | Dirty water rusts the machines | It is only done to save money
exercise: How does a zip close? | The slider pushes the teeth so each hooks into the gap opposite | Magnets inside the teeth pull them together | Glue on the teeth sticks them shut | The slider ties each pair of teeth together with a fine nylon thread
exercise: What happens to clothes put in a clothes bank? | They are sorted: resold, sent abroad, or made into rags | They are all burnt straight away | They all go back to the factory to be unpicked and sewn again | They are all buried in the ground
exercise: How is a picture screen-printed on a T-shirt? | Ink is pushed through a mesh screen, one screen per colour | The picture is painted on by hand with a tiny brush, colour by colour | It is ironed on from a newspaper | The picture is woven into the cloth with coloured threads
exercise: Each T-shirt design has 3 colours and needs one screen per colour. How many screens for 4 designs? | 12 | 7 | 3 | 4
test: «zip's lost a tooth, just yank it, it'll be fine» What will probably happen? | It will keep coming apart there, because the teeth can't hook | It will close tighter than before | The zip will slowly grow a new tooth | Nothing, zips work just as well with a gap where a tooth used to be
test: «ow, caught me skin in the zip» What is the safe rule? | Pull zips slowly and keep skin away from the teeth | Next time yank it up as fast as you can so it's over quick | Only zip up with your eyes shut so you don't flinch | Only zip up when the clothes are wet so the teeth slide
test: «my nan knits, so shop jumpers are knitted by nans» What is the true version? | Machines with hundreds of hooked needles do it with the same looping idea | Every shop jumper is hand-knitted by grannies | Shop jumpers are always woven on looms | Shop jumpers are moulded from plastic
test: «T-shirt was 3 quid, who cares where it's made» What is the clever answer? | Cheap clothes can hide low pay, unsafe factories and heavy water use | A low price just shows that hardly any work at all went into making it | Cheap T-shirts grow ready-made on cotton plants | Nobody works on cheap clothes at all
test: What would happen if jeans were woven with blue threads both ways? | The inside would be as dark blue as the outside | The jeans would turn white the first time they were washed | The jeans could not be sewn together | The jeans would come out twice as long
test: «sheep must hate being sheared, poor things» What is right? | Done properly, shearing is like a haircut and keeps sheep cool in summer | Shearing pulls all the wool out by the roots, so it always hurts the sheep | Sheep get ill after every shearing | Sheep are sheared every single day
test: «sewing machine works like my hands tying knots, just faster» What is right? | No, a hook below loops the top thread round a second thread | Yes, it has little metal fingers inside that tie a knot each time | Yes, but it ties only one big knot at the end | No, it glues the cloth together
test: Your old jumper has holes. Can it go in the clothes bank? | Yes, worn clothes can become rags or stuffing | No, only brand-new clothes with labels are allowed in | No, clothes with holes in them are always burnt | Yes, they sew new jumpers together out of the holes
test: Each trainer has 7 eyelets on each side. How many eyelets are on a pair of trainers? | 28 | 14 | 7 | 21
test: «T-shirt picture's all cracked, the factory painted it wrong» What is most likely? | Washing and stretching crack the printed ink layer over time | The picture was drawn with chalk | The cotton slowly eats the ink | Cracking just shows that the T-shirt is a cheap fake from a market
test: A sewing machine makes 5,000 stitches a minute. How many stitches in 2 minutes? | 10,000 | 5,002 | 7,000 | 2,500
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the interior surface of denim is paler than its exterior. | The fabric is woven with blue-dyed threads in one direction and undyed threads in the other | The interior is bleached by the factory subsequent to sewing | The interior is faded through contact with the wearer | The dye is applied by brush to the exterior alone
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the mechanism of a sewing machine? | A hook beneath the fabric loops the upper thread around a second thread, locking each stitch | The needle ties a small knot upon each passage through the fabric, as a hand would when sewing | Each stitch is fixed by the application of adhesive | The threads are fused together by heat
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the Rana Plaza disaster of 2013. | A clothing-factory building collapsed in Bangladesh, killing more than one thousand workers | A cotton harvest failed in Vietnam owing to drought | A zip factory was destroyed by fire in Europe | A ship laden with jeans sank in the Atlantic
exam: Determine the aggregate number of stitches executed by a machine operating at five thousand stitches per minute over a period of three minutes. | Fifteen thousand | Five thousand and three | Eight thousand | Fifty thousand
